WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.730
For men, I like to see
about a one-to-one ratio

00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:04.260
for the bench press such
that you should be able

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:06.000
to bench press your body weight.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.980
So a 200-pound person should be aiming

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:09.900
for something like 200-pound bench press.

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:12.360
For women, it's about .6.

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:14.310
Upper body strength is significantly less

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:16.680
in women in general, and
so you're going to have

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:18.720
to scale that down a little bit more.

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.570
Now for the back squat, it's
hard to give you numbers

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:23.310
because it is so technically demanding.

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:25.050
I think it's easier to give you something

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:27.570
more like a leg press value.

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:28.590
For men, you want to shoot

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:30.090
for something like double body weight

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:33.240
and for women something
like 1 1/2 times or so.

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:35.940
So that hopefully gives
you a little bit of context

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:37.710
of the numbers to go after.

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:40.680
The last thing I want to add
here is actually something

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:43.230
we'll talk about a lot more in the future,

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:44.063
and that is grip strength.

00:00:44.063 --> 00:00:45.540
It is incredibly important

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:48.270
and a great insight
into your overall health

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:50.313
for both athletes and non-athletes.

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:52.110
Easy to test.

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:53.700
There's a number of ways

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:55.680
that you can grab hand grip dynamometers.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:58.323
They're cheap and
available almost anywhere.

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.920
For men, just again, a rough number here.

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:05.550
I like to see individuals
over 45 kilograms

00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:08.730
and then for women over
28 kilograms or so.

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:10.170
Another thing to keep in mind here

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:12.810
is asymmetry really matters.

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:14.460
Now this is the first test I've given you

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:15.780
where you can isolate, you know,

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:17.520
your left side from your right side.

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:20.400
Really important to test
your grip strength in both.

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:23.940
Recent papers have come out
suggesting that an asymmetry

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:28.940
of more than 10% specifically
increases your risks

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:31.620
of sarcopenia and heightened denervation

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:34.380
of muscle groups by 2.67 fold.

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:37.320
And so we will talk about
that more in detail later.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:40.320
But really, really important,
you want to understand

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:41.970
whether or not you have major asymmetries

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:44.490
in strength between both your sides.

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:46.290
Again, some asymmetry is probably okay,

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:49.560
maybe even advantageous for
some sporting applications.

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:53.010
But asymmetries outside of that
are potentially concerning.

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:54.600
If you're unfamiliar with that term,

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:57.300
sarcopenia describes the excessive loss

00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:59.280
of muscle mass with age.

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:00.750
We know there's going to be some sort

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:03.750
of natural decline in the
amount of muscle mass you have.

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:08.700
In fact, on rough average,
men will lose about 40%

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:12.150
of their muscle mass from
the ages of 25 to 80.

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:13.380
So we know what's going to happen.

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:15.240
We've talked about how strength training

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:18.990
and adequate nutrition can
reduce that loss of muscle,

00:02:18.990 --> 00:02:20.040
but some of it's going to happen.

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:22.350
So sarcopenia really
refers to the excessive

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:24.240
or additional loss of muscle

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:26.310
beyond the normal loss with aging.

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:29.670
So those strength standards I
gave you are rough guidelines,

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:31.380
but I want you to get
as strong as possible.

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:33.750
I don't necessarily
need you to be squatting

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:35.820
or benching 1,300 pounds,

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:37.950
but I don't want you to just
stop at body weight either.

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:40.380
So outside of, of
course, leading to injury

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:42.810
and getting hurt, which is
something we don't want to do,

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:45.150
there's no disadvantage
to getting stronger.

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:47.070
I've tried to make that argument

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:49.710
with muscle mass earlier in the show,

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:52.980
but I can really make that
heavily with strength.

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:56.040
In fact, if you were to
compare the two, muscle mass

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:59.430
to muscle strength,
muscle strength is by far

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:02.610
a stronger predictor of both
how long you're going to live

00:03:02.610 --> 00:03:06.300
as well as how well you will
live within those years.

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:07.133
That's number one.

00:03:07.133 --> 00:03:10.230
Number two, getting
stronger only continues

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:12.060
to reduce your risk of developing things

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:15.210
like sarcopenia or late onset dementia.

00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:17.580
In fact, one particular
study of note here,

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:19.500
this study had around 500,000

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:21.900
or so individuals pulled
from the UK Biobank,

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:24.960
which is a similar setup as
what we've got here in America

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:28.470
that I talked about earlier
in our NHANES database.

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:31.290
Now, they studied these
individuals across nine years,

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:33.930
and during that time, around 4,000 or so

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:36.390
of the individuals developed dementia.

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:38.100
And so what's interesting
is they were able to go back

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:40.710
and say, okay, what unique
characteristics existed

00:03:40.710 --> 00:03:44.640
prior to the dementia as
well as after the onset?

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:46.170
And how did that compare
to the individuals

00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:48.033
who didn't have the dementia set in?

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:51.210
And in this one certain study,

00:03:51.210 --> 00:03:53.190
in just one study, so
we want to be cautious

00:03:53.190 --> 00:03:58.020
of overinterpreting here,
what they found was 30%

00:03:58.020 --> 00:03:59.970
of the dementia cases were attributed

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:02.220
to having low grip strength,

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:04.080
and this was independent of a number

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:06.390
of other important confounders.

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:09.630
We will talk about these
things more in future episodes,

00:04:09.630 --> 00:04:11.460
and I don't want you
to get overly concerned

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:14.610
with that specific result
or those specific numbers.

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:16.860
I just wanted to highlight,
though, the critical importance

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:21.270
of getting stronger really
helps overall global health.

00:04:21.270 --> 00:04:23.760
And what's furthermore
interesting about this paper

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:25.740
and many, many, many others

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:28.020
is there doesn't seem
to be an upper limit.

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:31.050
So when you continue to
increase strength you continue

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:34.530
to reduce risk of all-cause
mortality, dementia,

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:36.330
and similar other things.

00:04:36.330 --> 00:04:38.790
And so we don't see this
really even an asymptote.

00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:40.560
We don't see a tapering off of benefit.

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:43.860
We just continue to see
rises in improvements

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:48.270
in risk reduction factors,
hazard risk, mortality risk,

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:50.040
a number of other things important

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:51.690
to overall health when we
continue to get stronger,

00:04:51.690 --> 00:04:52.890
whether we're talking about grip strength,

00:04:52.890 --> 00:04:54.840
leg strength, or anything else.

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:57.840
And so it just, right now,
according to the data across,

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:02.550
again, multiple areas, just
continuing to get stronger

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:05.927
seems to continually benefit
human health and performance.

00:05:05.927 --> 00:05:08.344
[music]

